```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

In this example, a multiple regression model is fitted
using program quality ratings (`QUALITY`) as the regressand/outcome variable
and number of published articles attributed to the program faculty members (`NARTIC`),
percent of faculty members holding research grants (`PCTGRT`), and
percentage of program graduates who received support (`PCTSUPP`) as regressor/predictor variables
using a data set from 1982 ratings of 46 doctoral programs in psychology in the USA [@NationalResearchCouncil-1982].
Confidence intervals for the standardized regression coefficients are generated
using the `BetaMC()` function from the `betaMC` package.

```{r}
#| message = FALSE
library(package = betaMC)
```

```{r}
#| echo = FALSE
if (!exists(x = "nas1982")) {
  try(
    expr = utils::data(
      "nas1982",
      package = "betaMC"
    ),
    silent = TRUE
  )
}
df <- nas1982
```

```{r}
#| eval = FALSE
df <- betaMC::nas1982
```

### Regression

Fit the regression model using the `lm()` function.

```{r}
object <- stats::lm(formula = QUALITY ~ NARTIC + PCTGRT + PCTSUPP, data = df)
```

### Monte Carlo Sampling Distribution of Parameters

#### Normal-Theory Approach

```{r}
mvn <- MC(object = object, type = "mvn")
```

#### Asymptotic distribution-free Approach

```{r}
adf <- MC(object = object, type = "adf")
```

#### Heteroskedasticity Consistent Approach (HC3)

```{r}
hc3 <- MC(object = object, type = "hc3")
```

### Standardized Regression Slopes

#### Normal-Theory Approach

```{r}
mvn <- BetaMC(object = mvn)
```

#### Asymptotic distribution-free Approach

```{r}
adf <- BetaMC(object = adf)
```

#### Heteroskedasticity Consistent Approach (HC3)

```{r}
hc3 <- BetaMC(object = hc3)
```

## Methods

### summary

Summary of the results of `BetaMC()`.

```{r}
summary(object = mvn)
summary(object = adf)
summary(object = hc3)
```

### coef

Return the vector of estimates.

```{r}
stats::coef(object = mvn)
stats::coef(object = adf)
stats::coef(object = hc3)
```

### vcov

Return the sampling covariance matrix.

```{r}
stats::vcov(object = mvn)
stats::vcov(object = adf)
stats::vcov(object = hc3)
```

### confint

Return confidence intervals.

```{r}
stats::confint(object = mvn, level = 0.95)
stats::confint(object = adf, level = 0.95)
stats::confint(object = hc3, level = 0.95)
```

